    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p class=\"TableTitle\"><strong>Box&nbsp;2.03</strong> Summary of key genetic abnormalities identified in oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Marnix Jansen",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 446.55pt; background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"595\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 446.55pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"595\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Genetic factors</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 187.45pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 446.55pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 187.45pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"595\">\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><em><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">TP53</span></em><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\"> mutations are found in 59&ndash;93% of all cases <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{24670651;24686850;25151357}</span></span><span class=\"TableTextChar\">; i</span>n most cases, this is a key driver mutation developing at the stage of intraepithelial neoplasia (preinvasive carcinoma)</span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Mutations in other cell-cycle regulatory genes, such as <em>CDKN2A</em> (<em>P16</em>), <em>RB1</em>, <em>NFE2L2</em>, <em>CHEK1</em>, and <em>CHEK2</em>, have also been identified <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{24670651;24686850;25151357}</span></span>, as have amplifications of cell-cycle regulatory genes (<em>CCND1</em>, <em>CDK4</em>, <em>CDK6</em>, and <em>MDM2</em>)</span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Amplification or mutation of <em>EGFR</em> in 19% of tumours and alterations of <em>PIK3CA</em>, <em>PTEN</em>, or <em>PIK3R1</em>, all of which are believed to activate the PI3K pathway, in 24% of tumours <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{28052061}</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Single-base substitutions&nbsp;2 and&nbsp;13, suggesting that APOBEC activation is a crucial step in tumour development <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{34663923}</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Chromosome aneuploidy (in particular, gains in chromosomes&nbsp;3, 10, 12, and&nbsp;20) <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{28757263;28052061}</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.15pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 446.55pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"595\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Epigenetic factors</span></strong></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.15pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 446.55pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"595\">\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">DNA methylation, chromatin modification, RNA editing, and loss of genomic imprinting are common in oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{28052061;28757263}</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 17.15pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 446.55pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 17.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"595\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Inherited factors</span></strong></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 68.9pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 446.55pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 68.9pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"595\">\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Tylosis, an autosomal dominant disorder caused by a missense mutation in <em>RHBDF2</em>, results in overactivation of the <em>EGFR</em> signalling pathway <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{22265016}</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk210035107;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Fanconi anaemia, an autosomal recessive disorder, affects the Fanconi anaemia pathway, which is normally active in DNA repair <span class=\"Ref-ID\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">{19686080}</span></span></span></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
